The Audit Logging section in `plugins/index.md` shows an annotation sample that is missing essential properties. The current sample:

    ```cds
    annotate my.Customers with @PersonalData {
      ID           @PersonalData.FieldSemantics: 'DataSubjectID';
      name         @PersonalData.IsPotentiallyPersonal;
      email        @PersonalData.IsPotentiallyPersonal;
      creditCardNo @PersonalData.IsPotentiallySensitive;
    }
    ```

    This is incomplete — the `@PersonalData` annotation requires both `DataSubjectRole` and `EntitySemantics` properties to be specified. Without these, the Audit Log plugin silently writes no audit log events (no error or warning is produced). The correct annotation syntax has the `@PersonalData` annotation include a structured value with these two properties, followed by the field-level annotations in the body.

    Update the sample in `plugins/index.md` so it matches the working syntax shown in the [@cap-js/audit-logging README](https://github.com/cap-js/audit-logging#readme). This will prevent users from copying a sample that appears correct but silently fails to produce audit logs.

## Environment Setup
This is a documentation-only change in the VitePress-based docs site. Check `package.json` for the project setup and available scripts, and look at `CONTRIBUTING.md` for contribution guidelines. The target file to modify is `plugins/index.md`.

## Implementation Plan
- Locate the Audit Logging sample in the `plugins/index.md` file (in the `## Audit Logging` section).
    - Update the `annotate my.Customers with @PersonalData {` line to `annotate my.Customers with @PersonalData : {` and add the two required properties `DataSubjectRole` and `EntitySemantics` inside the annotation body, before the closing `}`.
    - Verify the resulting code block shows the complete, working annotation structure as documented in the plugin's own README.
    - If applicable, check whether any other samples in the file (e.g., in the Node.js section at the top) show the same incomplete pattern and update them for consistency (only if they reference the same `@PersonalData` annotation).

The parser currently fails with an "unknown table" error when a projector is declared with `AFTER INSERT` (or other table actions) on a workspace descriptor. For example, this schema should be valid:

    ```
    APPLICATION app1();
    WORKSPACE RestaurantWS (
        DESCRIPTOR Restaurant ();
        EXTENSION ENGINE BUILTIN (
          PROJECTOR NewRestaurantVat AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE ON (Restaurant) STATE(AppSecret, Http) INTENTS(SendMail);
        );
    );
    ```

    but it currently fails during `BuildAppSchema` because the resolver treats the descriptor name as a table reference and can't find it. The goal is to make the parser recognize workspace descriptors as valid projector trigger targets, so they are resolved correctly instead of producing an "unknown table" error. Unrelated/really-unknown references should still produce an error, so this must be a targeted change in the projector analysis logic, not a relaxation of error checking overall.

## Environment Setup
The repository is a Go module. Check `go.mod` and `go.work` at the repository root for the module layout and any workspace settings. The parser package lives in `pkg/parser/` — its own tests are in that directory. There are also CI workflow files under `.github/workflows/` (e.g. `ci-full.yml`, `ci-pkg-cmd.yml`) if you want to see how the project is built and tested in the pipeline, but for local work the standard Go tooling with the module files should suffice.

## Implementation Plan
1. Open `pkg/parser/impl_analyse.go` and locate the `analyseProjector` function. It is responsible for resolving the QName of each trigger target when table actions (like `AFTER INSERT`) are present.
    2. Before the existing table-resolution logic runs for a trigger target, attempt to look up the QName as a `*WsDescriptorStmt` using `lookupInCtx` (which is already used elsewhere in the parser for resolving statements).
    3. If the lookup succeeds and returns a non-nil descriptor, append the descriptor's own name as a `QName` to `trigger.qNames` (using the package's qualified name) and skip the existing table-resolution path. This makes the descriptor a valid, resolved trigger target.
    4. If the lookup fails (returns an error or a nil descriptor), fall through to the existing table resolution logic so that genuinely unknown references still produce the appropriate "undefined" errors.
    5. To make this possible, `lookupInCtx` and `resolveInCtx` in `pkg/parser/utils.go` must accept `*WsDescriptorStmt` in their type parameter unions. Add `*WsDescriptorStmt` to the union in both functions' generic constraints.
    6. Add a helper test assertion in `pkg/parser/impl_test.go` so tests can assert that a schema is valid without expecting specific errors. (The existing `AppSchemaError` helper can be refactored to expose the underlying error, and a new `NoAppSchemaError` method added.)
    7. Add regression tests: one that verifies the original buggy case now parses without error (using the descriptor name as a projector trigger), and one that ensures a truly unknown table name still produces an "unknown" error.

When a form contains a container component (such as tabs, grid, hbox, panel, service, or container) that itself contains another form, modifying field values in the inner form automatically triggers submission of the outer form. This causes unexpected behavior for users.

    For example, in the following schema:
    ```
    {
      type: 'page',
      body: {
        type: 'form',
        api: '/api/mock2/form/saveForm?pushCode',
        controls: [
          {
            type: 'tabs',
            tabs: [
              {
                title: 'json editor',
                body: {
                  type: 'form',
                  controls: [
                    {
                      name: 'json',
                      type: 'editor',
                      language: 'json'
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    }
    ```

    When the user edits the 'json' field in the inner form, the outer form's onChange handler is incorrectly triggered, causing a submission attempt.

    The root cause is that the container components within a form are passing the `onChange` prop down to nested forms, which then triggers the parent form's change handler. The solution is to exclude `onChange` from the props passed by these container components to their children.

    Please ensure that container components used within forms (Container, Grid, HBox, Panel, Service, Tabs) do not propagate the `onChange` handler to nested forms, preventing unwanted parent form submissions.

## Environment Setup
Check the repository's package.json and tsconfig.json for build configuration. The project uses TypeScript. Look at the existing test structure in `__tests__/` directory and the form renderer tests under `__tests__/renderers/` for testing patterns. Documentation for development setup can be found in docs/dev.md.

## Implementation Plan
<step>1. Identify the container components in `src/renderers/Form/` that render nested forms: Container.tsx, Grid.tsx, HBox.tsx, Panel.tsx, Service.tsx, and Tabs.tsx.</step>
    <step>2. For each of these components, add `'onChange'` to the static `propsList` array (where it doesn't already include it), following the pattern used by Grid.tsx and HBox.tsx.</step>
    <step>3. For components that don't currently have a `propsList` (Container, Panel, Service, Tabs), add a new static `propsList` property containing `['onChange']` (and `['onChange', 'tabs']` for Tabs).</step>
    <step>4. Verify that the `onChange` prop is now excluded from props passed down to nested forms within these container components.</step>
    <step>5. Test the scenario described above to confirm that modifying an inner form no longer triggers the outer form's submission.</step>
